course: dictionary
lesson: 12465
title: Definite: sure and clear
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Definite means clear, sure and fixed, with no doubt. If something is definite, it has been decided and nobody is guessing. «The party is on Saturday at three o'clock.» That sentence is definite, because the day and time are fixed.
explain: Compare it with «The party might be on Saturday, or maybe Sunday.» Might and maybe show doubt, so the plan is not definite yet. Look for clue words like sure, fixed and decided to spot a definite plan or answer.
explain: In grammar, definite has a special job. The word «the» is called the definite article, because it points to one particular thing. «The dog barked» means one dog we both know about. «A dog barked» could mean any dog at all, so «a» is an indefinite article.
fact: Definite means clear, sure and fixed, with no doubt.
fact: «A definite plan» is one that has been decided.
fact: «A definite yes» is a clear, sure yes, with no maybe about it.
fact: «The» is the definite article, because it points to one particular thing.
fact: «A» and «an» are indefinite articles, because they do not point to one particular thing.
exercise: «The bus leaves at half past eight, and that is definite.» What does definite mean here? | Maybe, or possibly | Broken | Very fast | *Sure and fixed, with no doubt
exercise: Which word is the definite article in «I saw the moon last night»? | saw | I | night | *the
exercise: Which sentence shows a definite plan? | *We are going to the park at two o'clock on Friday. | Perhaps we will go somewhere nice. | We might go to the park, or maybe not. | Who knows what we will do?
test: «Nadia said there was nothing definite yet about the trip.» What does that mean? | The trip is cancelled for ever | *The trip has not been decided or made sure yet | The trip is already booked and sure | The trip is at the seaside
test: Which of these is a definite answer? | I'm not sure, ask me later | Maybe, I think so | Perhaps, if it rains | *Yes, I will come to your party.
exam: Which word means sure and fixed, with no doubt? | Vague | Unsure | *Definite | Hidden
